  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Breezy and colder weekend with rain and snow \nshowers downwind of Lake Erie. \n\nOngoing cold air advection (CAA) downwind of the Lower Great\nLakes via W/NW low level wind trajectories will result in a \ncontinued expansion of lake effect and terrain enhanced \nrain/snow streamers through tonight. We increased % chance and \nduration of POPs and added to snow amounts along Laurel Mtn \nwhere favorable Huron connection could bring 2-3\" to the summits\nand ski resorts by Sunday night. Snow accumulations elsewhere \nover the higher terrain of the NW Allegheny Plateau will \ngenerally be less than an inch. Brisk NW winds 20-30 mph through\nthe weekend will add a winter-like chill factor with daytime \ntemps feeling no better than 30-40 degrees. \n\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Another extended stretch of dry and seasonal \nweather next week.\n\nLake effect rain and snow showers come to an end by Monday \nmorning as an upper level ridge builds into the region. High \npressure at the surface and aloft will provide dry conditions \nwith mainly clear skies for next week along with moderating \ntemperatures. Highs should reach the 55-65F range by the second\nhalf of next week or +10-15 degrees above the historical average\nfor mid November. Light winds, low humidity, and abundant\nsunshine will be ideal for any outdoor activities. Whether\nraking leaves, putting out holiday decorations, or enjoying time\noutside, it will be an ideal stretch of weather.",
